Signed-off-by: Chenyang Zhong <zhongcy95@gmail.com>

sm8750-common: sensors: remove agc_gain

I am not sure if it is necessary on sm8750.

Signed-off-by: Chenyang Zhong <zhongcy95@gmail.com>

sm8750-common: sensors: use the estimated screen brightness for max hyst

The grayscale gamma is a more accurate estimation of the screen's impact
on the light sensor value. Using this as the max range hysteresis means
that a new light sensor correction is calculated when either of the
following is true.

1. The screen content remained constant, but the ambient light lux
   exceeds the range max.
2. The ambient light lux remained constant, but the screen content got
   brighter and the raw light sensor value exceeds the range max.

Signed-off-by: Chenyang Zhong <zhongcy95@gmail.com>

sm8750-common: sensors: remove rgbw_max_lux_div and rgbw_lux_postmul

Now that rgbw_poly has been remove, AlsCorrection no longer uses a
third order polynomial to calculate cumulative correction. As a result,
the postmul value is not used either.

In addition, I expect sm8750 devices to almost always get the
rgbw_max_lux from props instead of from als_cali, so AlsCorrection
should not need to further scale the max lux by postmul.

Therefore, drop rgbw_max_lux_div and the rgbw_lux_postmul that is
calculated from the division.

Signed-off-by: Chenyang Zhong <zhongcy95@gmail.com>

sm8750-common: sensors: polynomial curves mapping brightness to lux

Many devices do not have a linear mapping between the panel brightness
and the illuminance lux measured by the ambient light sensor. There is
often an abrupt change in the curve after a certain brightness,
sometimes caused by HBM kicking in. In this case, the old logic of
linearly scaling the rgbw_max_lux by brightness no longer works.

This commit introduces another method of configuring the brightness to
lux mapping. When the brightness_breakpoint prop is non-zero,
AlsCorrection will use a polynomial equation to calculate the expected
illuminance lux for a fullwhite content. Then lux can then be scaled by
grayscale weights using values from RGB channels. The polynomials can be
second order (i.e. quadratic) or first order (linear), which should be
enough for most devices.

Signed-off-by: Chenyang Zhong <zhongcy95@gmail.com>

sm8750-common: sensors: drop the magic hysteresis of sensor update

AlsCorrection already uses an array of ranges to avoid frequent
screenshots. I'm not sure if we need another pair of magic numbers to
check the raw calibrated sensor readings. In addition, the framework
side also has 3 threshold arrays in config.xml to prevent fluctuations
in sensor readings from adjusting the screen brightness too frequently.

Signed-off-by: Chenyang Zhong <zhongcy95@gmail.com>

sm8750-common: als: unlock the captureResults buffer ASAP

Unlock the buffer immediately after everything we need has been read
from "out".

Signed-off-by: Chenyang Zhong <zhongcy95@gmail.com>

